  "worked_solution": "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set $∅$ is $\\{∅\\}.$ Therefore, $P(P(∅))=\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{∅\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}.$ Therefore, $P(P(\\{∅\\}))=\\{∅,\\{∅\\},\\{\\{∅\\}\\},\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set $\\{0\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{∅,\\{0\\}\\}.$ Therefore, $P(P(\\{0\\}))=\\{∅,\\{∅\\},\\{\\{0\\}\\},\\{∅,\\{0\\}\\}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
